---
/**
 * A short silent clip of a real venue, shown as a BAND — deliberately not as a
 * hero background.
 *
 * ## Why this is not behind the hero
 *
 * The brief asked whether the client's promo should sit behind the homepage
 * hero. It should not, and the reason is measured rather than felt.
 *
 * The hero is a midnight field carrying white display type, paper body copy and
 * an amber eyebrow — on solid `midnight` those measure 17.50:1, 16.09:1 and
 * 9.48:1. Put a video behind them and the ground stops being a token: it becomes
 * a composite that changes every frame, which is precisely the class of defect
 * `tokens:check` and axe cannot see (the tokens all stay correct; the composite
 * is what fails — the same shape as the unlayered-CSS bug that shipped every
 * button at 1.78:1 with every token in the file right).
 *
 * Sampling all 354 frames of `lakeville-promo.mp4`, the brightest block-averaged
 * region reaches Y=242/255 and even the DARKEST frame's brightest region is
 * Y=138 — the footage is mostly lit projection screens, so there is no frame
 * where the text area is reliably dark. Holding the existing contrast over the
 * worst frame needs a scrim of roughly **89% opacity** (the amber eyebrow is the
 * binding constraint; white alone needs 71%). At 89–90% the visible luminance
 * spread between the brightest and darkest frames collapses to about 0.01 —
 * i.e. you ship megabytes of video to render a texture nobody can see, and the
 * page pays for motion it does not get.
 *
 * Two further reasons, both structural:
 *
 * 1. The hero's committed idea is *"a scoreboard, not a brochure"* — hierarchy
 *    from weight and scale, never from decoration. A video ground is decoration.
 * 2. **The only promo footage is Lakeville's, and the hero is venue-neutral on
 *    purpose** (the venue question is asked at the Book click, not the front
 *    door). Lakeville's film behind a neutral hero quietly re-states the brand
 *    as Lakeville-with-an-annexe — the same asymmetry STYLE-GUIDE §6.10 exists
 *    to prevent.
 *
 * As a band the same footage costs nothing in contrast, because **no text is
 * composited over it**. The one control that does sit on the video takes a
 * solid amber fill with midnight ink (9.48:1, opaque), so its legibility is a
 * property of the button rather than of whatever frame is underneath.
 *
 * ## Playback contract
 *
 * - **Silent by construction.** The audio track is not muted, it is absent —
 *   the encode is `-an`. (The client's promos shipped with no audio stream at
 *   all; only the Stillwater walkthrough had one.)
 * - The clip fades in from and out to black, which is what makes it tile
 *   cleanly — the source is one continuous take that pans from a table to a
 *   tee, so its ends do not otherwise match.
 * - Everything else — lazy, in-view only, reduced-motion poster-only, pausable
 *   — is `lib/videoLoop.ts`, shared with `TechPillar`. It moved there when the
 *   second looping component arrived (FW-3992): a copy of an accessibility
 *   branch is a copy that eventually stops matching.
 */
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import SectionHead from './SectionHead.astro';
import { buttonClasses } from '../../lib/buttons';
import posterSource from '../../assets/images/lakeville-night-poster.jpg';

interface Props {
  heading: string;
  /** What the footage actually shows. */
  alt: string;
  caption: string;
  /** Basename in `public/video/`, without extension. */
  name: string;
}

const { heading, alt, caption, name } = Astro.props;

// The poster IS an image, so it goes through the asset pipeline; only the
// multi-megabyte video bypasses it and is served statically from `public/`.
const poster = await getImage({ src: posterSource, format: 'webp', width: 1280, quality: 70 });
---

<section class="on-dark bg-midnight">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-content px-gutter py-section lg:px-gutter-lg lg:py-section-lg">
    <SectionHead tone="dark">{heading}</SectionHead>

    <figure class="m-0" data-video-band>
      <div class="relative border-rule border-paper">
        <video
          class="block h-auto w-full"
          poster={poster.src}
          width="1280"
          height="720"
          preload="none"
          muted
          loop
          playsinline
          aria-label={alt}
          data-video
        >
          <source src={`/video/${name}.webm`} type="video/webm" />
          <source src={`/video/${name}.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
        </video>

        {/* Opaque amber fill — its contrast is its own, not the frame's. */}
        <button
          type="button"
          data-video-toggle
          class:list={[buttonClasses('sm', 'onDark'), 'absolute bottom-s4 left-s4']}
        >
          Play
        </button>
      </div>

      <figcaption class="mt-s5 max-w-prose-page text-small leading-body text-slate">{caption}</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  import { initVideoLoops } from '../../lib/videoLoop';
  initVideoLoops();
</script>
